{
  "slug": "artist:modeselektor",
  "name": "Modeselektor",
  "editorial_article": "Modeselektor is a seminal Berlin-based electronic music duo comprising Gernot Bronsert and Sebastian Szary. Emerging from the anarchic, post-Wall underground party scene of 1990s Berlin, the pair have become one of the most influential forces in contemporary German electronic music. Their name, taken from a function on the Roland Space Echo effects unit, reflects their foundational commitment to analog texture and sonic experimentation. Throughout their career, Bronsert and Szary have championed a distinct, genre-defying sound that they have jokingly labeled \"happy metal, hard rap, country-ambient, and Russian crunk,\" reflecting a stylistic fluidity that blends the jagged rhythms of IDM, the weight of bass music, and the driving energy of techno.\n\nTheir rise to prominence began in earnest following their association with Ellen Allien's BPitch Control label in the early 2000s, where they established themselves as key figures in the city's burgeoning scene. Their work is defined by high-profile collaborations with artists like Thom Yorke of Radiohead, Paul St. Hilaire, and Puppetmastaz, bridging the gap between underground electronic dance music and mainstream critical acclaim. In 2009, they launched their own influential label, Monkeytown Records, which became a vital hub for experimental electronic sounds, home to their output as well as projects by artists like Siriusmo and Mouse on Mars. Furthermore, their pivotal role as one-half of the collaborative supergroup Moderat, alongside Apparat, solidified their position as architects of a sophisticated, emotive, and rhythmically complex electronic aesthetic that resonates far beyond the dancefloor. Throughout their long-standing career, they have continuously evolved, moving from the rawness of their early BPitch releases to the more expansive, modular, and collaborative sounds found on their recent work, cementing their status as true pioneers of the modern Berlin sound.",
